Third-party source is vendored as SUBMODULES pinned to a commit or tag, never a
branch head, or as a RELEASE download verified against the checksum the project
published with it. vendor/manifest.json is the ledger `maic vendor` reads.

    vendor/ComfyUI
        https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI
        pinned at v0.38.0  (6b747c0428c343e1417219641db93a4fb7cb69ae)
        built by vendor/comfyui.sh: its own Python via uv, CUDA 13 torch,
        API nodes and auto-launch off, models on the external drive.

    vendor/comfyui-ollama
        https://github.com/stavsap/comfyui-ollama
        pinned at 6db7560576e5a59488708e6be13e07b5aba2432a  (no release tags upstream)
        patched by vendor/patches/comfyui-ollama-think.patch: the node passes
        `think` through to Ollama, so Qwen3.5 answers instead of reasoning until
        the token limit. `maic vendor add` applies it; already-applied is detected.

    vendor/lua-pins
        https://github.com/Cragady/lua-collection-nvim-pins.git
        pinned at 37bb6c600a9f16684db5d7d9e49b2bff23332d69
        The two Lua sources Neovim v0.12.2 pins, each with its own PROVENANCE,
        upstream url and sha256, and a verify.sh that fetches upstream, checks
        the hash and diffs the committed tree. MAIC builds
        luajit-2.1.1774638290/luajit (LuaJIT at fbb36bb6) through
        vendor/luajit.cmake, OUT OF TREE: the source is copied into the build
        directory and its own Makefile produces a static libluajit.a there. The
        submodule is never written to. The URL is https here (the collection is
        public); ColdFireEmu uses ssh for the same pin by choice.

    vendor/llama.cpp
        https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp
        pinned at b11284  (25747b08e7a0f9a59a2089ce6b98d2229b76042a)
        llama.cpp is MIT. Nothing from it is copied into MAIC; the submodule is
        the only source. Built by vendor/llamacpp.sh OUT OF TREE into
        <state>/vendor/llama.cpp-build (Release, GGML_CUDA when nvcc is found,
        LLAMA_OPENSSL=OFF so the binaries cannot fetch models over https):
        llama-server, llama-cli, llama-quantize, llama-gguf-split, linked in as
        <state>/vendor/llamacpp/bin. The model llama-server loads is the link
        <state>/vendor/llamacpp/current-model.gguf (`maic vendor use llamacpp PATH`).
        Tags are b<build number>; bump to the newest one, never a branch head.

    ollama  (release, not a submodule)
        https://github.com/ollama/ollama/releases  v0.35.0
        vendor/ollama.sh downloads the linux amd64 archive, fetches the
        release's sha256sum.txt, and refuses to unpack on a mismatch. Ollama is
        a Go + CUDA build; the official binary is the reproducible artefact.

WHY SUBMODULES AND NOT COPIES. A copy can be incomplete without anyone noticing
(a .gitignore written from a guess once dropped thirteen real source files from a
LuaJIT copy elsewhere; only a fresh clone would have shown it). A submodule's
tree is whatever the pinned commit says it is; we do not filter it, and the pin
is a hash rather than a copy someone might edit.

WHERE THE RUNNING COPIES LIVE. Not here. ~/.local/state/maic/vendor/<Name> is a
symlink to a checkout (this repo's, or one the user adopted with
`maic vendor adopt`), and ~/.local/state/maic/vendor/ollama/current points at a
release directory. Services and `maic artifacts` go through those links, so a
user with an existing install keeps it and nothing moves.

CHECKING

    git submodule update --init --recursive
    vendor/lua-pins/verify.sh                     # needs the network
    maic vendor                                   # what is linked and installed

BUMPING

Change the ref in vendor/manifest.json, move the submodule to that commit, and
say why in the commit message and here. Then `maic vendor add NAME` re-runs the
install script, which updates packages in place. Do not track a branch.
